1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,987
Welcome to School Computer Science,
Explained. This is AP Computer Science

2
00:00:04,987 --> 00:00:10,446
Principles, Big Idea 4: computer systems
and networks. We'll see how the internet

3
00:00:10,446 --> 00:00:15,568
moves data, why it keeps working when
parts fail, and how many computers can

4
00:00:15,568 --> 00:00:16,512
share one job.

5
00:00:17,685 --> 00:00:21,141
Big Idea 4 is about computer systems and
networks.

6
00:00:21,591 --> 00:00:24,066
Let's start with how the internet moves
data.

7
00:00:25,216 --> 00:00:31,170
A computing device is any machine that can
run a program, like a phone, a laptop, or

8
00:00:31,170 --> 00:00:31,808
a server.

9
00:00:32,258 --> 00:00:37,762
A computer network is a group of connected
devices that can send and receive data.

10
00:00:38,912 --> 00:00:43,157
The internet is a network of networks,
built on open protocols.

11
00:00:43,607 --> 00:00:49,799
A protocol is an agreed set of rules for
how data is sent and received. Open means

12
00:00:49,799 --> 00:00:53,122
anyone can use them, so any device can
join.

13
00:00:54,272 --> 00:00:58,984
Data is sent in packets. Each packet
carries a piece of the data, plus

14
00:00:58,984 --> 00:01:02,485
metadata, like where it's from and where
it's going.

15
00:01:02,935 --> 00:01:07,052
Packets can arrive out of order, and the
receiver puts them back together.

16
00:01:08,202 --> 00:01:13,987
Routing is finding a path from sender to
receiver. The path is chosen as the data

17
00:01:13,987 --> 00:01:16,416
travels, by routers along the way.

18
00:01:16,866 --> 00:01:21,939
Bandwidth is the most data that can be
sent in a fixed amount of time, measured

19
00:01:21,939 --> 00:01:23,159
in bits per second.

20
00:01:24,309 --> 00:01:29,384
The internet was designed to be scalable:
it can grow to handle more users and

21
00:01:29,384 --> 00:01:32,117
devices, and it keeps working as it grows.

22
00:01:33,267 --> 00:01:37,470
Your turn. Why is it important that
internet protocols are open?

23
00:01:37,920 --> 00:01:39,200
Pause the video now.

24
00:01:39,650 --> 00:01:44,367
Because anyone can use them, so devices
from any company can connect and

25
00:01:44,367 --> 00:01:45,154
communicate.

26
00:01:45,604 --> 00:01:49,188
Next, we'll look at TCP, IP, and HTTP.

27
00:01:50,338 --> 00:01:55,263
When you load a web page, several
protocols work together, each with its own

28
00:01:55,263 --> 00:01:55,522
job.

29
00:01:55,972 --> 00:01:57,124
They work in layers.

30
00:01:58,274 --> 00:02:03,178
IP, the internet protocol, gives each
device an address, and moves packets

31
00:02:03,178 --> 00:02:04,503
toward that address.

32
00:02:04,953 --> 00:02:08,772
But IP alone doesn't guarantee that every
packet arrives.

33
00:02:09,922 --> 00:02:14,701
TCP, the transmission control protocol,
makes delivery reliable.

34
00:02:15,151 --> 00:02:20,076
It numbers the packets, puts them in
order, and asks for any missing ones to be

35
00:02:20,076 --> 00:02:20,761
sent again.

36
00:02:21,911 --> 00:02:27,671
UDP is another option. It's faster, but it
doesn't check that every packet arrives.

37
00:02:28,121 --> 00:02:33,253
It's used for video calls and live games,
where speed matters more than a few lost

38
00:02:33,253 --> 00:02:33,753
packets.

39
00:02:34,903 --> 00:02:42,195
HTTP sits on top. It's the protocol for
requesting and sending web pages. HTTPS

40
00:02:42,195 --> 00:02:43,671
adds encryption.

41
00:02:44,121 --> 00:02:48,847
Each layer relies on the one below,
without needing to know its details.

42
00:02:48,847 --> 00:02:50,095
That's abstraction.

43
00:02:51,245 --> 00:02:57,430
The web and the internet aren't the same:
the World Wide Web is a system of linked

44
00:02:57,430 --> 00:03:00,674
pages that uses the internet, through
HTTP.

45
00:03:01,824 --> 00:03:08,504
Your turn. Which protocol makes sure
packets arrive and are put in order: IP,

46
00:03:08,504 --> 00:03:09,632
TCP, or HTTP?

47
00:03:10,082 --> 00:03:11,362
Pause the video now.

48
00:03:11,812 --> 00:03:18,084
TCP. IP handles addresses and routing, and
HTTP handles web pages.

49
00:03:18,534 --> 00:03:22,097
Next, fault tolerance: how the internet
survives failures.

50
00:03:23,247 --> 00:03:27,130
Cables get cut. Routers break. Yet the
internet keeps working.

51
00:03:27,580 --> 00:03:28,902
That's fault tolerance.

52
00:03:30,052 --> 00:03:34,959
A system is fault tolerant if it can keep
working even when some parts fail.

53
00:03:35,409 --> 00:03:40,764
The internet achieves this through
redundancy: extra paths and extra devices.

54
00:03:41,914 --> 00:03:45,690
Redundancy means there's more than one
path between devices.

55
00:03:46,140 --> 00:03:50,641
If one connection fails, data is routed
around it, through another path.

56
00:03:51,791 --> 00:03:57,207
On the AP exam, you'll see network
diagrams. A common question: which single

57
00:03:57,207 --> 00:04:01,839
connection, if broken, would stop two
devices from communicating?

58
00:04:02,289 --> 00:04:05,574
Look for a connection that is the only
path between them.

59
00:04:06,724 --> 00:04:11,695
Redundancy costs more, because extra
connections and devices must be built.

60
00:04:12,145 --> 00:04:17,436
But it makes the system more reliable, and
protects against single points of failure.

61
00:04:18,586 --> 00:04:23,981
Fault tolerance is used beyond networks
too: backup power, copies of data in

62
00:04:23,981 --> 00:04:26,820
several data centers, and spare servers.

63
00:04:27,970 --> 00:04:33,396
Your turn. In a network, devices A and B
connect only through router R. Is that

64
00:04:33,396 --> 00:04:35,181
connection fault tolerant?

65
00:04:35,631 --> 00:04:36,911
Pause the video now.

66
00:04:37,361 --> 00:04:43,173
No. If R fails, A and B can't communicate.
Adding a second path would make it fault

67
00:04:43,173 --> 00:04:43,804
tolerant.

68
00:04:44,254 --> 00:04:47,475
Next, we'll speed up computing with
parallel computing.

69
00:04:48,625 --> 00:04:51,185
Some jobs take too long on one processor.

70
00:04:51,635 --> 00:04:54,024
Splitting them up can make them much
faster.

71
00:04:55,174 --> 00:04:59,270
Sequential computing runs operations one
at a time, in order.

72
00:04:59,720 --> 00:05:04,763
Parallel computing splits a program into
parts, and runs some of them at the same

73
00:05:04,763 --> 00:05:06,568
time on different processors.

74
00:05:07,718 --> 00:05:11,153
A sequential solution's time is the sum of
all its steps.

75
00:05:11,603 --> 00:05:16,464
A parallel solution's time is the
sequential part, plus the longest of the

76
00:05:16,464 --> 00:05:19,091
parallel parts running at the same time.

77
00:05:20,241 --> 00:05:25,551
Example: three tasks take forty, thirty
and fifty seconds. Sequentially, that's

78
00:05:25,551 --> 00:05:27,366
one hundred twenty seconds.

79
00:05:27,816 --> 00:05:33,494
On two processors, put fifty on one, and
forty plus thirty on the other. The time

80
00:05:33,494 --> 00:05:35,667
is the larger: seventy seconds.

81
00:05:36,817 --> 00:05:40,870
Speedup is the sequential time divided by
the parallel time.

82
00:05:41,320 --> 00:05:46,120
Here, one hundred twenty divided by
seventy is about one point seven.

83
00:05:47,270 --> 00:05:52,023
Adding processors helps less and less,
because some steps must still happen in

84
00:05:52,023 --> 00:05:55,313
order, and processors may have to wait for
each other.

85
00:05:56,463 --> 00:06:02,020
Your turn. Tasks take twenty, twenty, and
twenty seconds. What's the parallel time

86
00:06:02,020 --> 00:06:04,527
on three processors, and the speedup?

87
00:06:04,977 --> 00:06:06,257
Pause the video now.

88
00:06:06,707 --> 00:06:12,382
All three run at once, so twenty seconds.
Sequential is sixty, so the speedup is

89
00:06:12,382 --> 00:06:15,219
sixty divided by twenty, which is three.

90
00:06:15,669 --> 00:06:17,461
Next, distributed computing.

91
00:06:18,611 --> 00:06:23,262
Some problems are too big for any single
computer, no matter how fast.

92
00:06:23,712 --> 00:06:27,360
So we use many computers, working together
over a network.

93
00:06:28,510 --> 00:06:33,139
Distributed computing uses multiple
devices to run a program.

94
00:06:33,589 --> 00:06:37,557
The devices can be in the same building,
or spread around the world.

95
00:06:48,454 --> 00:06:53,749
Search engines, weather forecasts and big
science projects all use distributed

96
00:06:53,749 --> 00:06:54,428
computing.

97
00:06:54,878 --> 00:06:59,486
Some projects even let volunteers donate
their computers' spare time.

98
00:07:00,636 --> 00:07:05,916
The challenge is coordination. Computers
must communicate over the network, which

99
00:07:05,916 --> 00:07:08,849
takes time, and work must be split
carefully.

100
00:07:09,999 --> 00:07:15,294
Parallel computing usually means many
processors in one machine. Distributed

101
00:07:15,294 --> 00:07:19,962
computing means many machines on a
network. Both split up the work.

102
00:07:21,112 --> 00:07:26,357
Your turn. A research project needs to
analyze more data than any one computer

103
00:07:26,357 --> 00:07:29,048
can store. What kind of computing helps?

104
00:07:29,498 --> 00:07:30,778
Pause the video now.

105
00:07:31,228 --> 00:07:36,142
Distributed computing: the data and work
are spread across many computers on a

106
00:07:36,142 --> 00:07:36,646
network.

107
00:07:37,096 --> 00:07:42,707
That's the end of Big Idea four. Next, Big
Idea five: the impact of computing.

108
00:07:43,857 --> 00:07:48,534
If this helped, subscribe to School
Computer Science, Explained, so you don't

109
00:07:48,534 --> 00:07:49,809
miss the next lesson.

110
00:07:50,259 --> 00:07:51,155
See you there.

